sn16.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn16.2:1.2 | S: At one time Venerable Mahākassapa and Venerable Sāriputta were staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: on one occasion Ven. Mahā Kassapa and Ven. Sāriputta were staying near Vārāṇasī in the Deer Park at Isipatana.
sn16.2:1.3 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Sāriputta came out of retreat, went to Venerable Mahākassapa, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta, emerging from his seclusion in the evening, went to Ven. Mahā Kassapa and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn16.2:1.4 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Mahākassapa: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Mahā Kassapa,
sn16.2:1.5 | S: “Reverend Kassapa, it’s said that without being keen and prudent you can’t achieve awakening, extinguishment, and the supreme sanctuary from the yoke. | T: “It is said, friend Kassapa, that a person without ardency, without compunction, is incapable of self-awakening, incapable of unbinding, incapable of attaining the unexcelled security from bondage.
sn16.2:1.6 | S: But if you are keen and prudent you can achieve awakening, extinguishment, and the supreme sanctuary from the yoke. | T: (none)
sn16.2:2.1 | S: To what extent is this the case?” | T: Now, how is a person without ardency, without compunction, incapable of self-awakening, incapable of unbinding, incapable of attaining the unexcelled security from bondage?
sn16.2:2.2 | P: kittāvatā ca panāvuso, ātāpī hoti ottappī bhabbo sambodhāya bhabbo nibbānāya bhabbo anuttarassa yogakkhemassa adhigamāyā”ti? | T: And how is a person ardent & compunctious capable of self-awakening, capable of unbinding, capable of attaining the unexcelled security from bondage?”
sn16.2:2.3 | S: “Reverend, take a mendicant who doesn’t foster keenness by thinking: ‘If unarisen unskillful qualities arise in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give up arisen unskillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give rise to unarisen skillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If arisen skillful qualities cease in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ | T: Ven. Mahā Kassapa: “There is the case, friend, where a monk, (thinking,) ‘Unarisen evil, unskillful qualities arising in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ arouses no ardency. (Thinking,) ‘Arisen evil, unskillful qualities not being abandoned in me…’ … ‘Unarisen skillful qualities not arising in me …’ … ‘Arisen skillful qualities ceasing in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ he arouses no ardency.
sn16.2:2.4 | S: That’s how you’re not keen. | T: This is how one is without ardency.
sn16.2:3.1 | S: And how are you not prudent? | T: “And how is one a person without compunction?
sn16.2:3.2 | S: Take a mendicant who doesn’t foster prudence by thinking: ‘If unarisen unskillful qualities arise in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give up arisen unskillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give rise to unarisen skillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If arisen skillful qualities cease in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ | T: There is the case where a monk, (thinking,) ‘Unarisen evil, unskillful qualities arising in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ feels no compunction. (Thinking,) ‘Arisen evil, unskillful qualities not being abandoned in me…’ … ‘Unarisen skillful qualities not arising in me …’ … ‘Arisen skillful qualities ceasing in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ he feels no compunction.
sn16.2:3.3 | S: That’s how you’re not prudent. | T: This is how one is without compunction.
sn16.2:3.4 | S: That’s how without being keen and prudent you can’t achieve awakening, extinguishment, and the supreme sanctuary from the yoke. | T: “This is how a person without ardency, without compunction, is incapable of self-awakening, incapable of unbinding, incapable of attaining the unexcelled security from bondage.
sn16.2:4.1 | S: And how are you keen? | T: “And how is one ardent?
sn16.2:4.2 | S: Take a mendicant who fosters keenness by thinking: ‘If unarisen unskillful qualities arise in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give up arisen unskillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give rise to unarisen skillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If arisen skillful qualities cease in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ | T: There is the case where a monk, (thinking,) ‘Unarisen evil, unskillful qualities arising in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ arouses ardency. (Thinking,) ‘Arisen evil, unskillful qualities not being abandoned in me…’ … ‘Unarisen skillful qualities not arising in me …’ … ‘Arisen skillful qualities ceasing in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ he arouses ardency.
sn16.2:4.3 | S: That’s how you’re keen. | T: This is how one is ardent.
sn16.2:5.1 | S: And how are you prudent? | T: “And how is one compunctious?
sn16.2:5.2 | S: Take a mendicant who fosters prudence by thinking: ‘If unarisen unskillful qualities arise in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give up arisen unskillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If I don’t give rise to unarisen skillful qualities, they’ll lead to harm.’ ‘If arisen skillful qualities cease in me, they’ll lead to harm.’ | T: There is the case where a monk, (thinking,) ‘Unarisen evil, unskillful qualities arising in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ feels compunction. (Thinking,) ‘Arisen evil, unskillful qualities not being abandoned in me…’ … ‘Unarisen skillful qualities not arising in me …’ … ‘Arisen skillful qualities ceasing in me would lead to what is unbeneficial,’ he feels compunction.
sn16.2:5.3 | S: That’s how you’re prudent. | T: This is how one is compunctious.
sn16.2:5.4 | S: That’s how if you’re keen and prudent you can achieve awakening, extinguishment, and the supreme sanctuary from the yoke.” | T: “This is how a person ardent & compunctious is capable of self-awakening, capable of unbinding, capable of attaining the unexcelled security from bondage.”
sn16.2:5.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn54.6
sn54.6:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn54.6:1.2 | S: There the Buddha … said: | T: There the Blessed One said,
sn54.6:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, do you develop mindfulness of breathing?” | T: “Monks, do you develop mindfulness of in-&-out breathing?”
sn54.6:1.4 | S: When he said this, Venerable Ariṭṭha said to him: | T: When this was said, Ven. Ariṭṭha replied to the Blessed One,
sn54.6:1.5 | S: “Sir, I develop mindfulness of breathing.” | T: “I develop mindfulness of in-&-out breathing, lord.”
sn54.6:1.6 | S: “But mendicant, how do you develop it?” | T: “But how do you develop mindfulness of in-&-out breathing, Ariṭṭha?”
sn54.6:1.7 | S: “Sir, I’ve given up desire for sensual pleasures of the past. I’m rid of desire for sensual pleasures of the future. And I have eliminated perception of aversion regarding phenomena internally and externally. | T: “Having abandoned sensual desire for past sensual pleasures, lord, having done away with sensual desire for future sensual pleasures, and having thoroughly subdued perceptions of resistance with regard to internal & external events,
sn54.6:1.8 | S: Just mindful, I will breathe in. Mindful, I will breathe out. | T: I breathe in mindfully and breathe out mindfully.”
sn54.6:1.9 | S: That’s how I develop mindfulness of breathing.” | T: (none)
sn54.6:2.1 | S: “That is mindfulness of breathing, Ariṭṭha; I don’t deny it. | T: “There is that mindfulness of in-&-out breathing, Ariṭṭha. I don’t say that there isn’t.
sn54.6:2.2 | S: But as to how mindfulness of breathing is fulfilled in detail, | T: But as to how mindfulness of in-&-out breathing is brought in detail to its culmination,
sn54.6:2.3 | S: listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: listen and pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn54.6:2.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” Ariṭṭha replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” Ven. Ariṭṭha responded to the Blessed One.
sn54.6:2.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
sn54.6:3.1 | S: “And how is mindfulness of breathing fulfilled in detail? | T: “And how, Ariṭṭha, is mindfulness of in-&-out breathing brought in detail to its culmination?
sn54.6:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant—gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut—sits down cross-legged, sets their body straight, and brings mindfulness to the present. | T: There is the case where a monk, having gone to the wilderness, to the shade of a tree, or to an empty building, sits down folding his legs crosswise, holding his body erect, and establishing mindfulness to the fore.
sn54.6:3.3 | S: Just mindful, they breathe in. Mindful, they breathe out. | T: Always mindful, he breathes in; mindful he breathes out.
sn54.6:3.4 | S: Breathing in heavily they know: ‘I’m breathing in heavily.’ Breathing out heavily they know: ‘I’m breathing out heavily.’ … | T: “[1] Breathing in long, he discerns, ‘I am breathing in long’; or breathing out long, he discerns, ‘I am breathing out long.’ [2] Or breathing in short, he discerns, ‘I am breathing in short’; or breathing out short, he discerns, ‘I am breathing out short.’ [3] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in sensitive to the entire body.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out sensitive to the entire body.’ [4] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in calming bodily fabrication.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out calming bodily fabrication.’ “[5] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in sensitive to rapture.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out sensitive to rapture.’ [6] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in sensitive to pleasure.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out sensitive to pleasure.’ [7] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in sensitive to mental fabrication.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out sensitive to mental fabrication.’ [8] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in calming mental fabrication.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out calming mental fabrication.’ “[9] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in sensitive to the mind.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out sensitive to the mind.’ [10] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in satisfying the mind.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out satisfying the mind.’ [11] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in steadying the mind.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out steadying the mind. [12] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in releasing the mind.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out releasing the mind.’ “[13] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in focusing on inconstancy.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out focusing on inconstancy.’ [14] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in focusing on dispassion [lit: fading].’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out focusing on dispassion.’ [15] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in focusing on cessation.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out focusing on cessation.’
sn54.6:3.5 | S: They practice like this: ‘I’ll breathe in observing letting go.’ They practice like this: ‘I’ll breathe out observing letting go.’ | T: [16] He trains himself, ‘I will breathe in focusing on relinquishment.’ He trains himself, ‘I will breathe out focusing on relinquishment.’
sn54.6:3.6 | S: This is how mindfulness of breathing is fulfilled in detail.” | T: “This, Ariṭṭha, is how mindfulness of in-&-out breathing is brought in detail to its culmination.”
sn54.6:3.7 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp4.14
snp4.14:1.1 | S: “Great seer, I ask you, the Kinsman of the Sun, | T: “I ask the Kinsman of the Sun, the Great Seer,
snp4.14:1.2 | S: about seclusion and the state of peace. | T: about seclusion & the state of peace.
snp4.14:1.3 | S: How, having seen, is a mendicant extinguished, | T: Seeing in what way is a monk unbound,
snp4.14:1.4 | S: not grasping anything in this world?” | T: clinging to nothing in the world?”
snp4.14:2.1 | S: “They would cut off the idea, ‘I am the thinker’,” | T: “He should put an entire stop to the root of objectification-classifications: ‘I am the thinker.’
snp4.14:2.2 | S: said the Buddha, | T: (none)
snp4.14:2.3 | S: “which is the root of all judgments <j>that emerge from proliferation. | T: (none)
snp4.14:2.4 | S: Ever mindful, they would train to remove | T: He should train, always mindful, to subdue any craving inside him.
snp4.14:2.5 | S: any internal cravings. | T: (none)
snp4.14:3.1 | S: Regardless of what things they know, | T: Whatever truth he may know,
snp4.14:3.2 | S: whether internal or external, | T: within or without,
snp4.14:3.3 | S: they wouldn’t be proud because of that, | T: he shouldn’t, because of it, make himself hardened,
snp4.14:3.4 | S: for that is not quenching, say the good. | T: for that isn’t called unbinding by the good.
snp4.14:4.1 | S: They wouldn’t let that make them conceited, | T: He shouldn’t, because of it,
snp4.14:4.2 | S: thinking themselves better or worse or alike. | T: think himself better, lower, or equal.
snp4.14:4.3 | S: When questioned in many ways, | T: Touched by contact in various ways,
snp4.14:4.4 | S: they wouldn’t keep justifying themselves. | T: he shouldn’t keep theorizing about self.
snp4.14:5.1 | S: A mendicant would be settled internally, | T: Stilled right within,
snp4.14:5.2 | S: and not seek peace from another. | T: a monk shouldn’t seek peace from another, from anything else.
snp4.14:5.3 | S: For one at peace inside themselves, | T: For one stilled right within,
snp4.14:5.4 | S: there’s no picking up, whence putting down? | T: there’s nothing embraced, so how rejected?
snp4.14:6.1 | S: Just as, in the middle of the ocean, | T: As in the middle of the sea
snp4.14:6.2 | S: no waves arise, it stays still; | T: it is still, with no waves upwelling,
snp4.14:6.3 | S: so too one unstirred is still— | T: so the monk—unperturbed, still—
snp4.14:6.4 | S: a mendicant would make <j>no pretension regarding anything.” | T: should not swell himself anywhere.”
snp4.14:7.1 | S: “He whose eyes are open has explained | T: “He whose eyes are open has described
snp4.14:7.2 | S: the truth he witnessed, <j>where adversities are removed. | T: the Dhamma he’s witnessed, subduing danger.
snp4.14:7.3 | S: Please now speak of the practice, sir, | T: Now tell us, sir, the practice:
snp4.14:7.4 | S: the monastic code and immersion in samādhi.” | T: the Pāṭimokkha & concentration.”
snp4.14:8.1 | S: “With eyes not wanton, | T: “One shouldn’t be careless with his eyes,
snp4.14:8.2 | S: they’d turn their ears from village gossip. | T: should close his ears to village-talk,
snp4.14:8.3 | S: They wouldn’t be greedy for flavors, | T: shouldn’t hunger for flavors,
snp4.14:8.4 | S: nor possessive about anything in the world. | T: or view anything in the world as mine.
snp4.14:9.1 | S: Though struck by contacts, | T: When touched by contact,
snp4.14:9.2 | S: a mendicant would not lament at all. | T: he shouldn’t lament,
snp4.14:9.3 | S: They wouldn’t pray for another life, | T: shouldn’t covet anywhere any states of becoming,
snp4.14:9.4 | S: nor tremble in the face of dangers. | T: or tremble at terrors.
snp4.14:10.1 | S: When they receive food and drink, | T: When gaining food & drink,
snp4.14:10.2 | S: edibles and clothes, | T: staples & cloth,
snp4.14:10.3 | S: they wouldn’t store them up, | T: he should not make a hoard.
snp4.14:10.4 | S: nor worry about not getting them. | T: Nor should he be upset when receiving no gains.
snp4.14:11.1 | S: Meditative, not footloose, | T: Doing jhāna, not footloose,
snp4.14:11.2 | S: they’d avoid remorse and not be negligent. | T: he should refrain from restlessness, shouldn’t be heedless,
snp4.14:11.3 | S: Then a mendicant would stay | T: should live in a noise-less abode.
snp4.14:11.4 | S: in quiet places to sit and rest. | T: (none)
snp4.14:12.1 | S: They wouldn’t take much sleep, | T: Not making much of sleep,
snp4.14:12.2 | S: but, being keen, <j>would apply themselves to wakefulness. | T: ardent, given to wakefulness,
snp4.14:12.3 | S: They’d give up sloth, illusion, mirth, and play, | T: he should abandon weariness, deception, laughter, sports,
snp4.14:12.4 | S: and sex and ornamentation. | T: sexual intercourse, & all that goes with it;
snp4.14:13.1 | S: They wouldn’t cast Artharvaṇa spells, <j>interpret dreams | T: should not practice casting spells, interpret dreams,
snp4.14:13.2 | S: or omens, or practice astrology. | T: physical marks, the stars,
snp4.14:13.3 | S: My followers would not study ominous animal cries, | T: animal cries;
snp4.14:13.4 | S: work as a doctor, or treat an impacted fetus. | T: should not be devoted to doing cures or inducing fertility.
snp4.14:14.1 | S: Not shaken by criticism, | T: A monk shouldn’t tremble at blame
snp4.14:14.2 | S: a mendicant would not gloat when praised. | T: or grow haughty with praise;
snp4.14:14.3 | S: They’d cast aside greed and stinginess, | T: should dispel stinginess, greed,
snp4.14:14.4 | S: anger, and slander. | T: divisive speech, anger;
snp4.14:15.1 | S: They’d not stand for buying and selling; | T: shouldn’t buy or sell
snp4.14:15.2 | S: a mendicant would not speak ill at all. | T: or revile anyone anywhere;
snp4.14:15.3 | S: They wouldn’t linger in the village, | T: shouldn’t linger in villages,
snp4.14:15.4 | S: nor cajole people from desire for profit. | T: or flatter people in hope of gains.
snp4.14:16.1 | S: A mendicant would be no boaster, | T: A monk shouldn’t boast
snp4.14:16.2 | S: nor would they speak suggestively. | T: or speak with ulterior motive,
snp4.14:16.3 | S: They wouldn’t train in impudence, | T: shouldn’t train in insolence
snp4.14:16.4 | S: nor speak argumentatively. | T: or speak quarrelsome words;
snp4.14:17.1 | S: They wouldn’t be led into lying, | T: shouldn’t engage in lies
snp4.14:17.2 | S: nor be deliberately devious. | T: or knowingly cheat;
snp4.14:17.3 | S: And they’d never look down on another | T: shouldn’t despise others for their
snp4.14:17.4 | S: because of livelihood, wisdom, or precepts and vows. | T: life, discernment, habits, or practices.
snp4.14:18.1 | S: Though provoked from hearing much talk | T: Annoyed on hearing many words
snp4.14:18.2 | S: from ascetics saying all different things, | T: from contemplatives or ordinary people,
snp4.14:18.3 | S: they wouldn’t react harshly, | T: he shouldn’t respond harshly,
snp4.14:18.4 | S: for the virtuous do not retaliate. | T: for those who retaliate aren’t calm.
snp4.14:19.1 | S: Having understood this teaching, | T: Knowing this teaching,
snp4.14:19.2 | S: inquiring, a mendicant would always train mindfully. | T: a monk inquiring should always train in it mindfully.
snp4.14:19.3 | S: Knowing quenching as peace, | T: Knowing unbinding as peace,
snp4.14:19.4 | S: they’d not be negligent in Gotama’s bidding. | T: he shouldn’t be heedless of Gotama’s message—
snp4.14:20.1 | S: For he is the vanquisher, the unvanquished, | T: for he, the Conqueror unconquered,
snp4.14:20.2 | S: seer of the truth as witness, not by legends— | T: witnessed the Dhamma, not by hearsay, but directly, himself.
snp4.14:20.3 | S: that’s why, being diligent, they would always train | T: So, heedful, you should always do homage & train
snp4.14:20.4 | S: respectfully in the Buddha’s teaching.” | T: in line with that Blessed One’s message,” the Blessed One said.
snp4.14:20.5 | P: Tuvaṭakasuttaṁ cuddasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.70
sn12.70:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn12.70:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn12.70:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was honored, respected, revered, venerated, and esteemed. And he received robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: Now at that time the Blessed One was worshipped, revered, honored, venerated, given homage—a recipient of robes, alms food, lodgings, & medical requisites for the sick.
sn12.70:1.4 | S: And the mendicant Saṅgha was also honored, respected, revered, venerated, and esteemed. And they received robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: The Saṅgha of monks was also worshipped, revered, honored, venerated, given homage—a recipient of robes, alms food, lodgings, & medical requisites for the sick.
sn12.70:1.5 | S: But the wanderers who followed other religions were not honored, respected, revered, venerated, and esteemed. And they didn’t receive robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: But the wanderers of other sects were not worshipped, revered, honored, venerated, or given homage, nor were they recipients of robes, alms food, lodgings, or medical requisites for the sick.
sn12.70:2.1 | S: Now at that time the wanderer Susīma was residing at Rājagaha together with a large community of wanderers. | T: Now at that time Susima the wanderer was living in Rājagaha with a large following of wanderers.
sn12.70:2.2 | S: Then his community said to Susīma, | T: And so Susima’s following of wanderers said to him,
sn12.70:2.3 | S: “Reverend Susīma, please lead the spiritual life with the ascetic Gotama. | T: “Come now, friend Susima. Go live the holy life under Gotama the contemplative.
sn12.70:2.4 | S: Memorize that teaching and have us recite it with you. | T: When you have completely mastered the Dhamma, tell it to us;
sn12.70:2.5 | S: When we’ve memorized it we’ll recite it to the laity. | T: when we have completely mastered it, we will teach it to householders
sn12.70:2.6 | S: In this way we too will be honored, respected, revered, venerated, and esteemed. And we’ll receive robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick.” | T: and then we, too, will be worshipped, revered, honored, venerated, given homage; we too will become recipients of robes, alms food, lodgings, & medical requisites for the sick.”
sn12.70:2.7 | S: “Yes, reverends,” replied Susīma to his own assembly. Then he went to Venerable Ānanda, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Responding, “As you say, friends,” to his own following, Susima the wanderer went to Ven. Ānanda and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.

