sn12.70:2.8 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Ānanda, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Ānanda,
sn12.70:2.9 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, I wish to lead the spiritual life in this teaching and training.” | T: “Friend Ānanda, I want to live the holy life in this Dhamma & Vinaya.”
sn12.70:3.1 | S: Then Ānanda took Susīma to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then Ven. Ānanda took Susima the wanderer to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn12.70:3.2 | S: “Sir, this wanderer Susīma says that | T: “Lord, this wanderer, Susima, has said,
sn12.70:3.3 | S: he wishes to lead the spiritual life in this teaching and training.” | T: ‘Friend Ānanda, I want to live the holy life in this Dhamma & Vinaya.’”
sn12.70:3.4 | S: “Well then, Ānanda, give Susīma the going forth.” | T: “Then in that case, Ānanda, give him the Going Forth.”
sn12.70:3.5 | S: And the wanderer Susīma received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: So Susima the wanderer gained the Going Forth in the presence of the Blessed One, he gained the Acceptance (into the Saṅgha of monks).
sn12.70:4.1 | S: Now at that time several mendicants had declared their enlightenment in the Buddha’s presence: | T: Now at that time a large number of monks had declared final gnosis in the Blessed One’s presence:
sn12.70:4.2 | S: “We understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: “We discern that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.’”
sn12.70:4.3 | S: Venerable Susīma heard about this. | T: Ven. Susima heard that
sn12.70:4.4 | P: “sambahulehi kira bhikkhūhi bhagavato santike aññā byākatā: | T: “A large number of monks, it seems, have declared final gnosis in the Blessed One’s presence:
sn12.70:4.5 | P: ‘khīṇā jāti, vusitaṁ brahmacariyaṁ, kataṁ karaṇīyaṁ, nāparaṁ itthattāyā’ti pajānāmā”ti. | T: ‘We discern that “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”’”
sn12.70:4.6 | S: He went up to those mendicants, and exchanged greetings with them. | T: Then Ven. Susima went to those monks and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with them.
sn12.70:4.7 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to those mendicants, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to them,
sn12.70:4.8 | S: “Is it really true that the venerables have declared enlightenment in the Buddha’s presence?” | T: “Is it true, as they say, that you have declared final gnosis in the Blessed One’s presence:
sn12.70:4.9 | P: ‘khīṇā jāti, vusitaṁ brahmacariyaṁ, kataṁ karaṇīyaṁ, nāparaṁ itthattāyā’ti pajānāmā”ti? | T: ‘We discern that “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world”’?”
sn12.70:4.10 | S: “Yes, reverend.” | T: “Yes, friend.”
sn12.70:5.1 | S: “But knowing and seeing thus, do you wield the many kinds of psychic power? That is, multiplying yourselves and becoming one again; materializing and dematerializing; going unobstructed through a wall, a rampart, or a mountain as if through space; diving in and out of the earth as if it were water; walking on water as if it were earth; flying cross-legged through the sky like a bird; touching and stroking with the hand the sun and moon, so mighty and powerful. Do you control the body as far as the realm of divinity?” | T: “Then, having known thus, having seen thus, do you wield manifold supranormal powers? Having been one you become many; having been many you become one? You appear? You vanish? You go unimpeded through walls, ramparts, & mountains as if through space? You dive in & out of the earth as if it were water? You walk on water without sinking as if it were dry land? Sitting cross-legged you fly through the air like a winged bird? With your hand you touch and stroke even the sun & moon, so mighty & powerful? You exercise influence with your body even as far as the Brahmā worlds?”
sn12.70:5.2 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn12.70:6.1 | S: “Well, knowing and seeing thus do you, with clairaudience that is purified and superhuman, hear both kinds of sounds, human and heavenly, whether near or far?” | T: “Then, having known thus, having seen thus, do you hear—by means of the divine ear-element, purified & surpassing the human—both kinds of sounds: divine & human, whether near or far?”
sn12.70:6.2 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn12.70:7.1 | S: “Well, knowing and seeing thus, do you understand the minds of other beings and individuals, having encompassed them with your mind? | T: “Then, having known thus, having seen thus, do you know the awareness of other beings, other individuals, having encompassed it with your own awareness?
sn12.70:7.2 | S: Do you understand mind with greed as ‘mind with greed’, | T: Do you discern a mind with passion as a mind with passion,
sn12.70:7.3 | S: and mind without greed as ‘mind without greed’? | T: and a mind without passion as a mind without passion;
sn12.70:7.4 | S: Do you understand mind with hate as ‘mind with hate’, | T: a mind with aversion as a mind with aversion,
sn12.70:7.5 | S: and mind without hate as ‘mind without hate’? | T: and a mind without aversion as a mind without aversion;
sn12.70:7.6 | S: Do you understand mind with delusion as ‘mind with delusion’, | T: a mind with delusion as a mind with delusion,
sn12.70:7.7 | S: and mind without delusion as ‘mind without delusion’? | T: and a mind without delusion as a mind without delusion;
sn12.70:7.8 | S: Do you understand constricted mind as ‘constricted mind’, | T: a restricted mind as a restricted mind,
sn12.70:7.9 | S: and scattered mind as ‘scattered mind’? | T: and a scattered mind as a scattered mind;
sn12.70:7.10 | S: Do you understand expansive mind as ‘expansive mind’, | T: an enlarged mind as an enlarged mind,
sn12.70:7.11 | S: and unexpansive mind as ‘unexpansive mind’? | T: and an unenlarged mind as an unenlarged mind;
sn12.70:7.12 | S: Do you understand mind that is not supreme as ‘mind that is not supreme’, | T: an excelled mind [one that is not on the most excellent level] as an excelled mind,
sn12.70:7.13 | S: and mind that is supreme as ‘mind that is supreme’? | T: and an unexcelled mind as an unexcelled mind;
sn12.70:7.14 | S: Do you understand mind immersed in samādhi as ‘mind immersed in samādhi’, | T: a concentrated mind as a concentrated mind,
sn12.70:7.15 | S: and mind not immersed in samādhi as ‘mind not immersed in samādhi’? | T: and an unconcentrated mind as an unconcentrated mind;
sn12.70:7.16 | S: Do you understand freed mind as ‘freed mind’, | T: a released mind as a released mind,
sn12.70:7.17 | S: and unfreed mind as ‘unfreed mind’?” | T: and an unreleased mind as an unreleased mind?”
sn12.70:7.18 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn12.70:8.1 | S: “Well, knowing and seeing thus, do you recollect many kinds of past lives? That is: one, two, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, a hundred, a thousand, a hundred thousand rebirths; many eons of the world contracting, many eons of the world expanding, many eons of the world contracting and expanding? Do you remember: ‘There, I was named this, my clan was that, I looked like this, and that was my food. This was how I felt pleasure and pain, and that was how my life ended. When I passed away from that place I was reborn somewhere else. There, too, I was named this, my clan was that, I looked like this, and that was my food. This was how I felt pleasure and pain, and that was how my life ended. When I passed away from that place I was reborn here.’ Do you recollect your many kinds of past lives, with features and details?” | T: “Then, having known thus, having seen thus, do you recollect your manifold past lives [lit: previous homes], i.e., one birth, two births, three births, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, one hundred, one thousand, one hundred thousand births, many eons of cosmic contraction, many eons of cosmic expansion, many eons of cosmic contraction & expansion, (recollecting), ‘There I had such a name, belonged to such a clan, had such an appearance. Such was my food, such my experience of pleasure & pain, such the end of my life. Passing away from that state, I re-arose there. There too I had such a name, belonged to such a clan, had such an appearance. Such was my food, such my experience of pleasure & pain, such the end of my life. Passing away from that state, I re-arose here’?”
sn12.70:8.2 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn12.70:9.1 | S: “Well, knowing and seeing thus, do you, with clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, see sentient beings passing away and being reborn—inferior and superior, beautiful and ugly, in a good place or a bad place—and understand how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds? ‘These dear beings did bad things by way of body, speech, and mind. They denounced the noble ones; they had wrong view; and they chose to act out of that wrong view. When their body broke up, after death, they were reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. These dear beings, however, did good things by way of body, speech, and mind. They never denounced the noble ones; they had right view; and they chose to act out of that right view. When their body broke up, after death, they were reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm.’ And so, with clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, do you see sentient beings passing away and being reborn—inferior and superior, beautiful and ugly, in a good place or a bad place. And do you understand how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds?” | T: “Then, having known thus, having seen thus, do you see—by means of the divine eye, purified & surpassing the human—beings passing away and re-appearing, and do you discern how they are inferior & superior, beautiful & ugly, fortunate & unfortunate in accordance with their kamma: ‘These beings—who were endowed with bad conduct of body, speech, & mind, who reviled the noble ones, held wrong views and undertook actions under the influence of wrong views—with the break-up of the body, after death, have re-appeared in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell. But these beings—who were endowed with good conduct of body, speech, & mind, who did not revile the noble ones, who held right views and undertook actions under the influence of right views—with the break-up of the body, after death, have re-appeared in a good destination, a heavenly world’?”
sn12.70:9.2 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn12.70:10.1 | S: “Well, knowing and seeing thus, do you have direct meditative experience of the peaceful liberations that are formless, transcending form?” | T: “Then, having known thus, having seen thus, do you dwell touching with your body the peaceful emancipations, the formless states beyond form [the formless jhānas]?”
sn12.70:10.2 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn12.70:11.1 | S: “Well now, venerables, how could there be such a declaration when these things are not attained?” | T: “So just now, friends, didn’t you make that declaration without having attained any of these Dhammas?”
sn12.70:11.2 | S: “Reverend Susīma, we are freed by wisdom.” | T: “We’re released through discernment, friend Susima.”
sn12.70:12.1 | S: “I don’t understand the detailed meaning of what you have said in brief. | T: “I don’t understand the detailed meaning of your brief statement.
sn12.70:12.2 | S: Please teach me this matter so I can understand the detailed meaning.” | T: It would be good if you would speak in such a way that I would understand its detailed meaning.”
sn12.70:12.3 | S: “Reverend Susīma, whether you understand or not, we are freed by wisdom.” | T: “Whether or not you understand, friend Susima, we are still released through discernment.”
sn12.70:13.1 | S: Then Susīma went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, | T: So Ven. Susima got up from his seat and went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he sat to one side.
sn12.70:13.2 | S: and informed the Buddha of all he had discussed with those mendicants. | T: As he was sitting there, he told the Blessed One the entire conversation he had had with those monks.
sn12.70:13.3 | S: “Susīma, first comes knowledge of the stability of natural principles. Afterwards there is knowledge of extinguishment.” | T: (The Blessed One said:) “First, Susima, there is the knowledge of the regularity of the Dhamma [dependent co-arising], after which there is the knowledge of unbinding.”
sn12.70:14.1 | S: “Sir, I don’t understand the detailed meaning of what you have said in brief. | T: “I don’t understand the detailed meaning of the Blessed One’s brief statement.
sn12.70:14.2 | S: Please teach me this matter so I can understand the detailed meaning.” | T: It would be good if the Blessed One would speak in such a way that I would understand its detailed meaning.”
sn12.70:14.3 | S: “Susīma, whether you understand or not, first comes knowledge of the stability of natural principles. Afterwards there is knowledge of extinguishment. | T: “Whether or not you understand, Susima, it is still the case that first there is the knowledge of the regularity of the Dhamma, after which there is the knowledge of unbinding.
sn12.70:15.1 | S: What do you think, Susīma? | T: “What do you think, Susima?
sn12.70:15.2 | S: Is form permanent or impermanent?” | T: Is form constant or inconstant?”—
sn12.70:15.3 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”—
sn12.70:15.4 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”—
sn12.70:15.5 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: “Stressful, lord.”—
sn12.70:15.6 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: “And is it fitting to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as:
sn12.70:15.7 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?”
sn12.70:15.8 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn12.70:15.9 | S: “Is feeling permanent or impermanent?” | T: “… Is feeling constant or inconstant?”—
sn12.70:15.10 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”
sn12.70:15.11 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.12 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: …
sn12.70:15.13 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.14 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.15 | S: “No, sir.” | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.16 | S: “Is perception permanent or impermanent?” | T: “… Is perception constant or inconstant?”—
sn12.70:15.17 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” … | T: “Inconstant, lord.” …
sn12.70:15.18 | S: “Are choices permanent or impermanent?” | T: “… Are fabrications constant or inconstant?”—
sn12.70:15.19 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”
sn12.70:15.20 | S: “But if they’re impermanent, are they suffering or happiness?” | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.21 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: …
sn12.70:15.22 | S: “But if they’re impermanent, suffering, and perishable, are they fit to be regarded thus: | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.23 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.24 | S: “No, sir.” | T: (none)
sn12.70:15.25 | S: “Is consciousness permanent or impermanent?” | T: “What do you think, Susima? Is consciousness constant or inconstant?”—
sn12.70:15.26 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”—
sn12.70:15.27 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”—
sn12.70:15.28 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: “Stressful, lord.”—
sn12.70:15.29 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: “And is it fitting to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as:
sn12.70:15.30 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?”
sn12.70:15.31 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn12.70:16.1 | S: “So, Susīma, you should truly see any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Thus, Susima, any form whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every form is to be seen as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn12.70:16.2 | S: You should truly see any kind of feeling at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> feeling—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any feeling whatsoever.…
sn12.70:16.3 | S: You should truly see any kind of perception at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> perception—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any perception whatsoever.…
sn12.70:16.4 | S: You should truly see any kind of choices at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> choices—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any fabrications whatsoever.…
sn12.70:16.5 | S: You should truly see any kind of consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any consciousness whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle, common or sublime, far or near: Every consciousness is to be seen as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn12.70:17.1 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: “Seeing thus, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness.
sn12.70:17.2 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn12.70:17.3 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn12.70:18.1 | S: Susīma, do you see that rebirth is a requirement for old age and death?” | T: “Susima, do you see that from birth as a requisite condition there is aging-&-death?”
sn12.70:18.2 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
sn12.70:18.3 | S: “Do you see that continued existence is a requirement for rebirth?” | T: “Do you see that from becoming as a requisite condition there is birth?”

